The '''2020 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2019–20 UEFA Europa League]], the 49th season of [[Europe]]'s secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 11th season since it was renamed from the UEFA Cup to the [[UEFA Europa League]]. It was played at the [[RheinEnergieStadion]] in [[Cologne]], Germany on 21 August 2020, between Spanish side [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]] and Italian side [[Inter Milan]]. The match was held [[Behind closed doors (sport)|behind closed doors]] due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Europe]].<ref name="BCD2020Final"/>

The final was originally scheduled to be played on 27 May 2020 at the [[Stadion Energa Gdańsk]] in [[Gdańsk]], Poland.<ref name="venue">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0255-0e99f554980e-63465ec08dbc-1000--2022-uefa-europa-league-final-to-be-held-in-seville/ |title=Seville to host 2021 UEFA Europa League final |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=24 September 2019 |access-date=28 September 2019}}</ref> However, UEFA announced on 23 March 2020 that the final was postponed due to the COVID-19 pandemic.<ref>{{cite news|date=23 March 2020|title=UEFA Club Finals postponed|website=[[UEFA]]|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025b-0f8e76c8c787-b9f310787e70-1000--uefa-club-finals-postponed/|access-date=23 March 2020}}</ref> On 17 June 2020, the UEFA Executive Committee chose to relocate the final to Cologne, as part of a "final-eight tournament" consisting of single-match knockout ties played in four stadiums across Germany.<ref name="ExCo 17 June">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025e-0fb6101cfdbf-8b878372d79d-1000--uefa-competitions-to-resume-in-august/ |title=UEFA competitions to resume in August |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 June 2020 |access-date=17 June 2020}}</ref>

Sevilla won the match 3–2 for their record sixth UEFA Cup/Europa League title. As winners, they earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League]], [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]], in the [[2020 UEFA Super Cup]]. They also qualified to enter the group stage of the [[2020–21 UEFA Champions League]]; since Sevilla had already qualified through their league performance, the berth reserved was given to the third-placed team of the [[2019–20 Ligue 1]] ([[Stade Rennais FC|Rennes]]), the 5th-ranked association according to next season's access list.<ref name="uefa1819">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0242-0e16a85014cb-5ffe48223b95-1000--champions-league-and-europa-league-changes-next-season/ |title=Champions League and Europa League changes next season |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=27 February 2018 |access-date=27 February 2018}}</ref>

==Venue==
{{for|the details on the selection of Gdańsk as the original host for the final|2021 UEFA Europa League Final#Host selection}}
[[File:FIFA WM06 Stadion Koeln.jpg|thumb|275px|The [[RheinEnergieStadion]] in [[Cologne]] hosted the final.]]

The UEFA Executive Committee chose [[RheinEnergieStadion]] in [[Cologne]] as the host at their meeting on 17 June 2020.<ref name="ExCo 17 June"/> This was the first UEFA club competition final hosted at the stadium and the first Europa League final held in Germany since [[2010 UEFA Europa League Final|2010]]. During the two-legged final era, the country hosted either one or both legs 11 times, before hosting the single-legged [[2001 UEFA Cup Final]] in Dortmund and the 2010 final in Hamburg.

The stadium was first opened in 1923 as the Müngersdorfer Stadion and has been the home stadium of German [[Bundesliga]] side [[1. FC Köln]] since 1948. It underwent two major renovations during its lifetime. It hosted [[UEFA Euro 1988]] as well as the [[2005 FIFA Confederations Cup]] and the [[2006 FIFA World Cup]].

==Background==
The match was a record-extending sixth UEFA Cup/Europa League final for [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], the most successful team in competition history. The club won all their prior finals in [[2006 UEFA Cup Final|2006]], [[2007 UEFA Cup Final|2007]], [[2014 UEFA Europa League Final|2014]], [[2015 UEFA Europa League Final|2015]] and [[2016 UEFA Europa League Final|2016]].<ref name="rsssf">{{cite web |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec3b.html |title=UEFA Cup |work=[[RSSSF]] |date=28 February 2019 |access-date=17 August 2020}}</ref>

[[Inter Milan]] reached their fifth UEFA Cup/Europa League final, second only to Sevilla. They previously won three finals in [[1991 UEFA Cup Final|1991]], [[1994 UEFA Cup Final|1994]] and [[1998 UEFA Cup Final|1998]], and lost to [[FC Schalke 04|Schalke 04]] in [[1997 UEFA Cup Final|1997]]. They were the first Italian team to reach a UEFA Cup/Europa League final since [[Parma Calcio 1913|Parma]] in [[1999 UEFA Cup Final|1999]].<ref name="rsssf"/>

